>> B6: Describe a potential race in thread_set_priority() and explain
>> how your implementation avoids it.  Can you use a lock to avoid
>> this race?

Potential scenario with a race condition:

- Thread A calls the thread_set_priority() function
- Code goes through Thread A's donation list and is about to update its priority
- Interrupt happens and causes a context switch
- Thread B attempts to acquire a lock held by Thread A and 
    donates its priority to Thread A
- Program modifies Thread A's donation list
- Context switches back to Thread A
- Thread A sets its priority based on the old list 
    - Program counter has already 
    passed the "read donation" list line
- Thread B's donated priority is now lost

How our implementation deals with this:

Our implementation disables interrupts before changing 
any priority or checking the donation list.
This is due to the fact that priority donation
occurs in the lock_acqure function which can be called
from another thread. Disabling interrupts prevents
Pintos from context switching while the program reads 
and wries the priority of the caller thread 

Can you use a lock to avoid this race?:

No using locks would not avoid this race. thread_set_priority can be 
called while a thread already holds a lock and so using an additional 
lock could cause a deadlock. This is because locks depend on the priority 
information being correct to work properly, therefore using a lock to 
protect the priority information that locks depend on would result in a 
circular dependency where neither can safely run without the other being 
correct.


---- RATIONALE ----

>> B7: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

1. 
Priority Donation using donations_list in thread struct.
We chose to implement a linked list for each thread that a list
of all the threads donating to it, as well as a waiting_lock field 
that stores a pointer to the lock that the thread is waiting for.

Another design we considered was having an int donation_priority field 
for each thread that would update if another donor thread had a higher
priority. 

Our implementation is superior because donations_list can hold multiple locks 
and receive donations from multiple threads, whereas a single integer cannot 
keep track of which donation came from which lock/thread. Furthermore, if the  
thread loses it's highest donor and consequently should lose it's priority, 
donation_priority has no way of tracing that interaction. 

2. 
We insert in donations_list in sorted order and keep it sorted throughout all 
functions. The alternative would be an unsorted list that you would have to 
iterate through to find the maximum.

Similar to the decision for sleep_list, although insertion 
increases to O(n) from O(1), when we need to find the highest priority, 
our time complexity decreases to O(1) from O(nlogn) 
(O(nlogn) would be from sorting). 

Since we modify donation_list way less often than reading the 
max priority, the tradeoff results in an overall more effecient program.

3.
Our waiting list for semapores, locks, and condvar are inserted normally
without ordering, and then sorted when we need to get the highest 
priority waiter.

Contrasting our donations list and sleep list which are kept sorted,
we decided to not keep waiters for semaphores, locks and condvar in 
sorted order and instead sort when needed. This would make our inserts 
O(1) instead of O(n) but make whenever we needed to get the next highest 
priority waiter take O(nlogn) from sorting.

We did this for a few reasons.

First of all our implementation of priority donation for threads is
done within locks and our lock implementation functions. Our locks 
all have priority donation logic embedded, but semaphores or condvar 
do not have the same. This means semaphore's and condvar's waiter's 
priorities may change without us being able to keep the waiter list 
sorted. As such, to make sure semaphores and condvar work properly 
in future contexts or in general, we do a simple insert and sort.
Although it may be slightly slower it is much safer, more modular,
and more future proof.

Also unlike a sleep list which is checked on every tick semaphores,
condvar checks (for the highest priority waiter) happen much
less frequently, therefore the total O(nlogn) sorts would not be as costly.

4.
We also designed our preemption checks (checking if current thread needs
to yield to a higher priority thread) so that it isn't within the
thread_unblock function. 

We could have added an extra check within thread_unblock to make preemption
easy, however we thought it would be poor design. Thread_unblock is a very 
integral function that is used in many places within an os where interrupts 
are off, or yielding mid-way would be unsafe. 

So to keep thread_unblock modular and safe to use in all contexts we instead
checked preemption only in cases where we know it is safe. For example we 
check preemption within thread_create only after the new thread is added to 
the ready list. We check preepmtion within sema_up only after the highest 
priority waiter is added to the ready list.

Our design choice makes sure thread_unblock is simple, modular, future proof,
and safe, while still making sure we preempt at the right times.


